---
title: "Interactive and explorable documents"
description: "Documents a reader can operate: reactive js cells, inputs without boilerplate, scrollytelling, narrated code walkthroughs, tabbed panels and 3-D."
---

Taliesin runs `{js}` cells in the browser and wires them into a small reactive
graph. On top of that graph sit two declarative conveniences, inputs and
scroll-driven scenes, plus two scroll and tab layouts. Everything here is
client-side and offline, built from the same block model as the rest of the page,
and the preview stays read-only: a reader interacts with the output, never the
source.

The examples below are shown as source listings. Each feature has a runnable
counterpart in the corpus (`corpus/reactive/`, `corpus/explorable/`,
`corpus/narrate/`, `corpus/layout/`).

## Reactive `{js}` cells

A `{js}` cell declares its place in a dependency graph with leading `//|`
directives (they must come before any code):

- `//| viewof: NAME` makes the cell an input control published under `NAME`.
- `//| name: NAME` publishes the cell's return value under `NAME`.
- `//| input: A, B` consumes those names; the cell re-runs when any of them change.

Inside a cell, read values through the injected `tali` API: `tali.value("k")` returns
a control's current value, and `tali.get("squared")` returns the last value a
`//| name` cell published. A first cell publishes a slider as `n`:

```js
//| viewof: n
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range"; input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.value = "3";
return input;
```

A second derives a value from it, and a third consumes that derived value, so the
chain `n` to `squared` to the readout stays in sync transitively:

```js
//| name: squared
//| input: n
return tali.value("n") ** 2;
```

```js
//| input: squared
return document.createTextNode("n squared = " + tali.get("squared"));
```

When an input changes, Taliesin re-runs exactly the cells downstream of it, once
each, in dependency order, following `//| name` edges. This is one controlled pass,
not a continuously-recomputing dataflow engine: cycles are detected and shown as an
error rather than run. `Plot`, `d3` and `num` are available as globals for drawing; a cell
returns a DOM node (mounted for you) or builds into `tali.container`, and an
`invalidation` promise lets long-running cells (animations, WebGL) tear down before
a re-run.

### Numerics: the `num` global

Beside `Plot` and `d3` sits `num`, a small curated numerics namespace, so a cell that
wants a probability density does not hand-roll one:

```js
const xs = num.linspace(-4, 4, 200);
return Plot.plot({
  marks: [Plot.line(xs, { x: (d) => d, y: (d) => num.gaussian.pdf(d, 0, 1) })],
});
```

It holds distributions (`gaussian`/`normal`, `gamma`, `beta`, `poisson`, `exponential`,
each with `pdf`/`pmf` and `cdf`), summary statistics (`mean`, `variance`, `sd`, `median`,
`quantile`, `sum`), grids (`linspace`, `range`), small dense linear algebra (`matmul`,
`matvec`, `transpose`, `identity`, `cholesky`, `inv2`, `eig2`) and a **seeded** generator.

The seed is the point of `num.random`:

```js
const rng = num.random(20260729);
const draws = rng.sample(400, () => rng.normal(0, 1));
```

A published explorable that resamples on every render is not reproducible — the prose says
"note the outlier on the right" and the outlier is gone. `Math.random()` cannot be seeded,
so anything that samples should come from here. Values are plain arrays throughout, so
they cross straight into `Plot` and `d3` with no conversion.

`num` is deliberately small: it is what a *drawing* cell needs, not a numerical library.
Anything wanting more than a Cholesky or a 2×2 eigendecomposition belongs in a `{python}`
cell, where there is a real kernel.

### Showing a value: `tali.tex` and `tali.table`

A cell that computes a number usually wants to *show* it. Two builders turn a value into
output over the same DOM-return contract:

```js
//| input: fitted
return tali.tex(tali.get("fitted").cov, { label: "Σ̂" });
```

`tali.tex(value, { label, digits })` typesets a number, a vector (a 1-D array) or a matrix
(a 2-D array), using the same typeface as the `$math$` in your prose. It is **not** a TeX
engine and takes no TeX: real mathematics stays `$\Sigma = L L^\top$` in the text, and this
exists so a computed posterior precision reads as a quantity rather than as
`0.4472135954999579`.

`tali.table(rows, { columns, limit, digits })` renders an array of records (columns
inferred in first-seen order), an array of arrays, or an array of values. It shows 20 rows
by default and *says* when it is showing 20 of 5,000, rather than stopping silently.

### Accumulating across re-runs: `tali.state`

A cell body re-runs from scratch on every pass, which is what keeps this a scheduler rather
than a reactive virtual machine. For an iterative demo — an EM sweep, a descent trace —
that means there is nowhere to keep the thing being iterated. `tali.state` is the exception:

```js
//| input: t
let theta = tali.state("theta", { mu: 0, sigma: 1 });
theta = emStep(theta);
tali.setState("theta", theta);
return tali.tex([[theta.mu, theta.sigma]], { label: "μ, σ" });
```

Three limits are the design, not omissions. The store is **per cell**, so two cells using
`"theta"` do not collide. Writing it **schedules nothing**, so no write can start a
cascade. Its lifetime is the cell's: editing the cell clears it. To share a value *between*
cells, publish it with `//| name:` — an edge the graph can see and order — rather than
writing it here.

## Inputs without boilerplate: `{{< input >}}`

The `{{< input >}}` shortcode emits a labelled, keyboard-accessible control whose
value becomes a reactive node, so a `{js}` cell reacts with `//| input:` and no
control-building code. Five of them are ordinary form fields:

```markdown
{{< input name="k" type="slider" min="1" max="10" step="1" value="3" label="k" >}}
{{< input name="n" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="20" label="n" >}}
{{< input name="on" type="checkbox" value="true" label="enabled" >}}
{{< input name="q" type="text" value="hello" label="query" >}}
{{< input name="c" type="select" options="alpha,beta,gamma" value="beta" label="channel" >}}
```

`name` is required (it is the reactive node); `type` defaults to `slider`; `label`
defaults to the name. `tali.value()` returns a number for slider and number, a
boolean for checkbox, and a string for text and select. A consuming cell is then just
a `{js}` cell with `//| input: k` that reads `tali.value("k")`.

**`slider` is an alias of `range`.** Both render the same HTML `<input type="range">`
and both get a live `<output>` readout that updates as you drag, so the reader always
sees the current value beside the track. `number` shares their numeric `min`/`max`/
`step`/`value` attributes but renders as a spinner with no readout. The two names are
interchangeable; use whichever reads better in your source.

A **`select`** gives a closed set of choices. Its `options` are a comma-separated list,
and `options` is required for this type (omit it and `check` warns). `value` picks the
initially selected option:

```markdown
{{< input name="palette" type="select" options="viridis,magma,cividis" value="magma" label="Palette" >}}
```

That publishes a reactive node `palette` whose value is the chosen string. A chart cell
then reacts to it:

```js
//| input: palette
const name = tali.value("palette");          // "viridis" | "magma" | "cividis"
return Plot.plot({ color: { scheme: name }, marks: [/* … */] });
```

### Two controls that are not form fields

`animate` and `point` publish through the same reactive node as everything above; they
just are not a box you type in.

**`animate`** is a play / pause / step / reset control over a monotonic tick, for a demo
that advances a frame at a time: an EM sweep, a gradient descent, a travelling wave.

```markdown
{{< input name="t" type="animate" min="0" max="60" step="1" fps="10" label="frame" >}}
```

`min` is the starting value (and where Reset returns to), `step` is how far one frame
moves, `fps` is how fast Play advances, and `max` makes the tick **loop** back to `min`
rather than stop — a sweep that halts on its last frame reads as a hang. Leave `max` off
and it counts on forever. A consuming cell is an ordinary sink:

```js
//| input: t
const k = tali.value("t");                   // a number, not a string
return Plot.plot({ marks: [/* … drawn for frame k … */] });
```

Playback advances **one** scheduled pass per frame, and the next frame is not requested
until that pass has finished, so a slow cell slows the animation instead of queueing
passes behind itself.

**`point`** is a two-dimensional coordinate the reader drags, for "place a data point and
watch it refit". It is the one control whose value is structured: `tali.value()` returns
`{x, y}`.

```markdown
{{< input name="p" type="point" min="-3" max="3" step="0.2" value="0.8,1.2" label="new observation" >}}
```

`min`/`max` are the domain of **both** axes, `value` is `"x,y"` (default: the centre of
the domain), and `step` is how far one arrow-key press moves. **`y` increases upward**,
because these pads place points on plots. The pad is focusable and arrow-key operable, and
the readout beside it announces the new coordinates, so it works without a pointer.

Both controls ride the URL fragment like every other input, so a link captures the frame
you paused on or the point you dragged to.

## Shader cells: `{glsl}`

A `{glsl}` cell is a fragment shader drawn to a live `<canvas>`. Like `{js}`, its kernel
is the reader's browser: nothing runs at build time, nothing is vendored, and the whole
language costs no payload, because WebGL is already in the page.

The cell body is the shader's `main`. Taliesin supplies the vertex shader and a preamble,
so you write only the part that differs:

````markdown
```{glsl}
//| input: freq
void main() {
  vec2 p = (uv - 0.5) * 2.0;
  float v = 0.5 + 0.5 * sin(length(p) * u_freq - u_time * 2.0);
  gl_FragColor = vec4(v * 0.62, v * 0.55, v * 0.86, 1.0);
}
```
````

The preamble gives you:

| name | type | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `uv` | `varying vec2` | position in the canvas, `0..1`, y up |
| `u_res` | `uniform vec2` | canvas size in device pixels |
| `u_time` | `uniform float` | seconds since the cell mounted |
| `u_<name>` | `uniform float` or `vec2` | one per `//| input:` name |

An input whose value is a number arrives as a `float`; a `point` input arrives as a
`vec2`. **`u_time` only ticks when your shader mentions it** — a shader that does not is
drawn once and on each input change, and then costs nothing, so a page of static shaders
does not pin a core rendering pixels that never change.

A `{glsl}` cell takes `//| label:` and `//| fig-cap:` like any other, so a shader can be a
numbered, cross-referenceable figure. It participates in the same reactive graph, so
`//| name:` and `//| input:` mean exactly what they mean for `{js}`, and `check` reports a
dangling input in a shader the same way.

## Python in the browser: `{pyodide}`

A `{pyodide}` cell runs Python in the reader's browser: no Jupyter kernel, nothing
executed at build time. It is a separate fence from `{python}`, not a mode on it:
`{python}` runs against a warm kernel at build time, and the two never share state,
even side by side on the same page.

The runtime (Pyodide plus NumPy) is vendored and loads lazily: a cell starts fetching
it once the cell comes within about a screen of the viewport, so a reader who stays
well away from every `{pyodide}` cell downloads none of it. A cell near the top of a
page therefore starts loading as soon as the page opens, without any scrolling.

````markdown
```{pyodide}
#| name: samples
import numpy as np
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
rng.normal(size=500).tolist()
```
````

A cell's last expression is its published value; `#| name:` puts that value into the
same reactive graph a `{js}` or `{glsl}` cell publishes into, so a `{js}` cell
downstream reads it with `tali.get(...)`. The value is `null` until the runtime has
finished its first run, so a consumer should guard on that. `#| input:` works the
same way in reverse: the named values are injected into the Python namespace before
the cell runs, so the source reads them directly by name. A name that is not a valid
Python identifier (`my-slider`, say) still re-runs the cell but is *not* injected,
because it cannot be spelled in Python source; the same rule `{glsl}` applies to a
name that is not a valid uniform. `print` output is shown above the returned value,
in the same order a `{python}` cell uses.

Only the Python standard library and NumPy are available. Installing anything else
would need a network fetch, which Taliesin does not do, so `import scipy` fails with
Python's own traceback plus a note explaining that only the stdlib and NumPy ship
with the page.

A single self-contained HTML file cannot carry the 12.9 MB runtime, so
`taliesin build file.tmd out.html` (and `--out <dir>`, which also inlines its assets)
degrades a `{pyodide}` cell to a visible, highlighted source listing instead of
running it. The output modes where a `{pyodide}` cell actually runs are `taliesin
preview` and a multi-page **site** build (`taliesin build <dir>`), which copies the
runtime into `_assets/` beside the pages.

## Scrollytelling: `::: {.scrolly}`

A `.scrolly` container pins a visual stage while a column of `.step` scenes scrolls
past it. As each step crosses the middle of the viewport it becomes active: its
`state=` value is written to `data-scrolly-state` on the container (for pure-CSS
effects) and, when the container has a `name=`, into a reactive input of that name.
So a sticky `{js}` cell reacts with `//| input:`, the same graph as everything else.
Scrollytelling, then, is a reactive input driven by scroll position instead of a
slider.

```markdown
::: {.scrolly name="scene"}
[ a {js} cell with //| input: scene that draws the stage ]
::: {.step state="trend"}
First, the steady linear trend.
:::
::: {.step state="spike"}
Now the quadratic spike.
:::
:::
```

The stage is every non-`.step` block in the container; the narration is the `.step`
divs. The first step is active on load, and the input's initial value matches it, so
the cell has a sane value before any scroll. Omit `name=` for pure-CSS scenes.

## Narrated code walkthroughs: `::: {.code-walkthrough}`

A `.code-walkthrough` pins one code block as a sticky panel and scrolls `.step`
prose beside it. Each step's `lines=` focuses a line range in the panel, washing it in
the accent, so the reader's eye tracks the narration through the code.

```markdown
::: {.code-walkthrough}
[ one code block: the sticky panel ]
::: {.step lines="1"}
First we import NumPy.
:::
::: {.step lines="3-5"}
Then one EM iteration: an E-step, then an M-step.
:::
:::
```

The line spec is a comma-separated list of single lines and ranges, so one step can
focus several non-contiguous regions at once: a leading block plus a later call site,
say.

```markdown
::: {.step lines="1-5,8"}
The setup (lines 1 through 5) plus the one call site on line 8.
:::
```

Here `"1-5,8"` lights lines 1 through 5 and line 8 together and dims everything else.
Line numbers are 1-based and count the panel's own lines. Other valid specs are a
single line (`"3"`), a single range (`"3-5"`), or a longer mix (`"1,4,6-8"`). To show
the whole block with nothing dimmed, use `"all"` or leave `lines` off entirely; that is
the right choice for an opening "here is the finished code" step before you start
walking through it.

## Tabbed panels: `::: {.panel-tabset}`

A `.panel-tabset` turns its headings into tabs. Each heading at the shallowest level
present becomes a tab button (emitted as an ARIA `role="tab"`, not a real heading,
so it never pollutes the table of contents); the blocks under it become that tab's
panel. Here is one live:

::: {.panel-tabset}

## Python

Content for the Python tab.

## R

Content for the R tab.

:::

The first tab is selected on load. Readers switch with a click or the keyboard
(arrow keys move between tabs, Home and End jump to the first and last). Panel
content keeps its own ids and source positions, so click-to-source still works
inside a tab, and a `@fig-` reference resolves through it.

## Interactive 3-D with a `{js}` cell

A `{js}` cell mounts whatever DOM node it returns, and that node can be a WebGL
canvas. So a full interactive 3-D scene, spin, zoom, fullscreen, is just a cell that
imports a 3-D library and returns its renderer's canvas. The shape is always the same:

```js
const THREE = await import("https://esm.sh/three@0.163.0");

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(620, 500);

const scene  = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(45, 620 / 500, 0.1, 100);
camera.position.set(4, 3, 6);
scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 1));
scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(1.5, 0),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x4c8dff, flatShading: true }),
));

(function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
})();

invalidation.then(() => renderer.dispose());   // clean up when the cell re-runs

return renderer.domElement;   // the cell mounts the canvas
```

Two things make this idiomatic. `invalidation` is a promise the cell provides that
resolves when the cell is about to re-run or leave the page, so it is where you cancel
animation frames and dispose GPU resources. And because a cell returns a node rather
than printing markup, you can attach controls, an `OrbitControls` from three.js, a
`<select>`, sliders, straight to the same container.

::: {.callout-important}
## Gate teardown on `invalidation`, never on DOM attachment

The node a cell returns is mounted **after** the cell body finishes. Inside the body it
is not yet in the document, so a guard like this silently does nothing on the first
paint:

```js
const node = document.createElement("div");
if (!node.isConnected) return;      // always true here — the cell never renders
```

That reads like a defensive check and behaves like an early return. The same mistake
appears as an init gated on `offsetWidth`, on `getBoundingClientRect()`, or on a
`ResizeObserver` that has not fired yet: none of them has a useful answer until the node
is mounted, and none of them is how you learn the cell is finished. `invalidation` is,
and it works whether or not the node ever reaches the DOM. If you genuinely need
post-mount measurements (a canvas sized to its container), take them inside a
`requestAnimationFrame` callback, which runs after the mount.
:::

The data can come from a `{python}` or `{r}` cell. Compute coordinates or a mesh in
the kernel, `define()` them, and read them back in the `{js}` cell with
`tali.get("...")`: the numbers come from your kernel, the rendering is browser-native.

```js
// {python} cell:  define(points=my_array.tolist())
const points = tali.get("points");   // in the {js} cell
```

The **Live 3-D graphics** gallery exhibit is built entirely this way: a molecule
viewer, a Lorenz attractor, a CAD model beside a computed gear, and instrumented
sorting algorithms, each one a `{js}` cell in a plain `.tmd` file.
